Based on my analysis, the authorization gate for the user stories list endpoint is `CanViewUsFilterBackend` (permission `view_us`), which scopes the queryset to projects the requester can view. The new `EpicFilter` is a pure queryset-narrowing filter (adds `epics__in` / `epics__isnull` conditions) that runs *after* this permission filter in the `filter_backends` tuple, so it cannot widen the already-scoped queryset. The `epic` query param is only used to narrow results, not to fetch an arbitrary object by ID. The `attach_extra_info` call with `epic_id` is unchanged code (not part of this section's diff) and is not an authorization gate.

The only changed lines are the `BaseRelatedFieldsFilter` param_name refactor, the new `EpicFilter` class, and its registration in `filter_backends`. None of these introduce a missing or incorrect authorization check; the existing `view_us` permission filter remains the sole and effective authorization control on the list endpoint.

